---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LinkButton from '../components/LinkButton.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Karl Oberio | Technical Writer';
---

<BaseLayout
	pageTitle={pageTitle}
	description="Karl Oberio is a technical writer focused on practical API documentation, OpenAPI workflows, and developer-facing content."
	hideTitle={true}
>
	<section class="hero section-band">
		<div class="section-inner hero-grid">
			<div class="hero-copy">
				<p class="eyebrow">Karl Oberio</p>
				<h1>I write practical documentation for developer tools.</h1>
				<p class="hero-lede">
					I write API docs, OpenAPI guides, and developer-facing notes that keep the technical details intact while making the next step easier to see.
				</p>
				<div class="actions">
					<LinkButton link="/work/" text="Read samples" variant="primary" />
					<LinkButton link="mailto:yarn@docko.dev" text="Send an email" />
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="hero-panel" aria-label="Selected profile details">
				<p class="panel-label">Working around</p>
				<ul class="clean-list">
					<li>API documentation, onboarding guides, and reference context</li>
					<li>OpenAPI, SwaggerHub, Redoc, Markdown, and static docs sites</li>
					<li>Drafts that need a careful technical editor before they ship</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section-band section-band--muted">
		<div class="section-inner split-section">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">How I tend to work</p>
				<h2>Clear docs, written close to the product.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="strength-grid">
				<article>
					<h3>Structure</h3>
					<p>I separate setup, concepts, tasks, and reference material so readers are not forced to untangle everything at once.</p>
				</article>
				<article>
					<h3>Technical accuracy</h3>
					<p>I work from specs, product behaviour, and engineer feedback, then make the wording match what actually happens.</p>
				</article>
				<article>
					<h3>Tooling awareness</h3>
					<p>I am comfortable around Git, static sites, OpenAPI files, and the small build details that shape docs work.</p>
				</article>
				<article>
					<h3>Plain-language judgment</h3>
					<p>I keep the voice direct without sanding away terms that developers need to recognise.</p>
				</article>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section-band">
		<div class="section-inner">
			<div class="section-heading">
				<p class="eyebrow">Writing samples</p>
				<h2>A few pieces of docs work.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="work-grid">
				<article class="work-card">
					<p class="card-kicker">API documentation</p>
					<h3><a href="/work/api-docs-snippet/">API Docs Snippet</a></h3>
					<p>A compact introduction for a REST API product, covering authentication, errors, and response conventions.</p>
				</article>
				<article class="work-card">
					<p class="card-kicker">Docs workflow</p>
					<h3><a href="/work/redoc-openapi/">Building Redoc Output from OpenAPI Specs</a></h3>
					<p>A procedural guide that turns a SwaggerHub export into publishable Redoc output.</p>
				</article>
			</div>
			<p class="section-note">
				Coding projects are represented through GitHub for now. I am keeping formal coding case studies unpublished until there is enough useful context around the scope and outcomes.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section-band contact-band">
		<div class="section-inner contact-strip">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">Contact</p>
				<h2>Say hello about technical writing or docs projects.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="actions">
				<LinkButton link="mailto:yarn@docko.dev" text="yarn@docko.dev" variant="primary" />
				<LinkButton link="https://linkedin.com/in/docko" text="LinkedIn" />
				<LinkButton link="https://github.com/doc-ko" text="GitHub" />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
